mm/page_reporting: what a report holds is free memory

A page reporting pass takes free pages of page_reporting_order and up off
the free lists and holds them until the device has reported them: as many
as PAGE_REPORTING_CAPACITY of the largest blocks at once, 128 MiB with
4 KiB pages. For that time sysinfo(2) and /proc/meminfo show that much less
free and available memory, though the memory is as free as it was: a
guest's agent that set its tenants' share of memory by MemAvailable read
it during a pass and gave them none, and a program that sizes itself by the
memory it sees does the same. And an allocation that needs those pages
finds none: reclaim cannot free what the pass holds, and the OOM killer
ends a task in a guest whose memory is free. In a small guest, the first
pass, two seconds into its life, took nearly all of it.

Count the pages a pass holds as free in si_meminfo() and
si_mem_available(), and have an allocation that would otherwise give up
wait a tick and try again while a pass holds any (should_reclaim_retry()):
they come back as soon as the device has reported them. The zones' own
counts, by which the allocator and its watermarks see the pages gone until
they are back, are as they were. The count falls before a page is put back
and rises after it is taken, so a reader sees at most one block too little,
never too much. Reading page_reporting.held gives the count, in pages: what
a guest's agent reads to know that its kernel treats them so.

